-- Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One, four robots (printed 167-180): Scorpion Battler EX-V50, Sunfire NG-SE48, Super Labor Robot NG-V10, Super Max NG-8000
-- 4 vehicle rows, with 61 locations and 27 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~290-northern-gun-1-robots-f.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-ex-v50-scorpion-battler', 'Scorpion Battler EX-V50 Combat & Construction', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot, who drives and works the pincers, and a gunner sealed inside the top turret who runs the turret and tail guns; the pilot can take the tail weapons over so the two platforms engage different targets', 'Four in comfort, 6-8 if cramped', 'Running 60 mph (96 km) top speed; it cannot leap. Nothing can knock it over, knock it down or roll it, and a small rammer takes back whatever damage it deals. Losing two legs costs nothing; three lost across both sides costs 30% speed and -15% piloting; three lost from one side drags it at 70% less speed, -40% piloting, -2 initiative and parry and one fewer melee attack; with one leg left per side it still crawls at half speed and -30% piloting', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the bottom at 25 mph (40 km); maximum depth 2,000 ft (610 m)', 'Height 16 ft (4.9 m) riding low with the tail curled off to one side at turret height, 22 ft (6.4 m) with the tail curled over the turret; 4 ft (1.2 m) of ground clearance when walking. Width 18 ft (5.5 m). Length 22 ft (6.7 m) from nose to the end of the main body, plus 12 ft (3.6 m) for the tail in its usual curled position and 5 ft (1.5 m) for the pincers, which reach 15 ft (4.6 m); laid flat, the tail is 36 ft (11 m) long', '28', 350, 1, NULL, 30000000, '38 million credits nuclear (15 year life), 30 million with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell; no electric battery version. The lowest is stored. Fair to good availability. A turretless explorer and construction model, the EX-EC49 Scorpion Wanderer, sells for 28 million nuclear or 22 million with a fuel cell (fair availability).', 'Model Type: EX-V50. Class: All-Terrain Robot Combat Vehicle. Northern Gun''s answer to Bandito Arms'' Tarantula ATR (itself copied by Titan Robotics as the Spider and by the Manistique Imperium as the Black Widow), with a nod to the Coalition''s Scorpion Skull-Walker; designed by Doug Roy, Brian Terrill, Orion Pobursky, Carmen Bellaire and Sam Beedah and released in spring 109 P.A. An eight-limbed walker (six legs plus two pincers) built for war and for long stays in the wilds alike: two crew run it, four more ride along, and the body holds barracks bunks, a small kitchen and dining area, a shower and an ion-vaporizing toilet. The jointed tail ends in a weapons platform with an ion cannon, twin lasers, two mini-missile tubes, a gun camera and a pair of spotlights, and it can point anywhere, swat foes or be used to hit tall opponents a pincer cannot reach; a turret behind the cockpit adds more guns. The left pincer grabs, lifts, digs, crushes and impales; the right is a big cutting saw on a swiveling flipper for doors, walls, fences, trees and other robots; both serve for construction, trenching, logging, salvage and hauling. A backhoe folds out of the nose for earthmoving. Buyers like that it rides low and that real scorpions scare people. Forward section: destroying the cockpit section halts the legs, pincers, backhoe, cockpit controls and main sensors, but the robot is not dead: the power plant sits in the midsection, which is the main body, so the turret (turning 360 degrees, with its own half-range sensor and radio suite and camera) and the tail keep fighting until the midsection reaches zero. Windows: the cockpit plexiglass (25 M.D.C. each) is covered in combat by sliding alloy plates (50 more each) at a button or voice command, the pilot then driving on video from the nose or pincer cameras and instruments; a window takes a Called Shot at -3, and with glass and plate gone the crew is exposed. Robot P.S. 45. Cargo: a trunk and footlocker''s worth of space per bunk, a cargo closet 8 by 4 by 8 ft (2.4 by 1.2 by 2.4 m), a refrigerator-sized specimen cabinet, and behind the pilot''s seat (and likewise in the turret) a rack for two rifles, two backpacks, a spare armor suit and basic gear. Power: nuclear, 15 year life; Solid Oxide Fuel Cell available. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), except: punches and tears per the pincers (weapon entries); tail punch or swat 4D6 M.D. (no tail power punch); stomp 1D6 M.D. against targets under 7 ft (2.1 m); no head butt, leaps or kicks; ram/body block standard. A pincer power punch or trip counts as two attacks. Pincer trip: a strike aimed at a giant''s lower legs that must be parried or dodged; if it lands the target falls, taking no damage but losing initiative and two attacks. Climbing pin: against a downed foe both roll initiative, the Scorpion at +2; if the foe wins he rises (still losing his two attacks), otherwise the Scorpion climbs on top and attacks with pincers and tail for the rest of the round at +2 to strike while the victim can parry but not dodge. Each later round the victim may try to throw it off, at a cost of two attacks per try, only with Robotic P.S. 40 or Supernatural P.S. 30 or better, at least half the Scorpion''s weight, and a won initiative roll; while on top the Scorpion is +2 on initiative, strike, parry, dodge and every combat maneuver against him. Sensors: the nose pod carries the standard robot set, the turret a backup at half range; four cameras (turret, tail, each pincer) feed the cockpit, turret, passenger area and a screen over each bunk, each reaching 4,000 ft (1,219 m) with telescopic and x10 macro lenses, 10,000 hours of storage, transmission, and motion detection to 100 ft (30.5 m); passive nightvision for pilot and gunner (2,000 ft/610 m, useless in total darkness); a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three; and two winches with hook and tow line, one in the tail''s rump and one hidden in the nose, for cargo, towing and climbing. The EX-EC49 Scorpion Wanderer drops the turret, carries only a medium laser (4D6 M.D., 2,000 ft/610 m) and a Vibro-Blade (3D6 M.D.) in the tail, and runs at 70 mph (112 km), otherwise the same.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.167-171'),
  ('ng-se48-sunfire', 'Sunfire Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'One pilot', 'Two, tightly; the pilot may let one of them fire the variable bore rail gun', 'Running 70 mph (112 km) maximum. Leaping 5 ft (1.5 m) high and 12 ft (3.6 m) across', 'Not possible', 'Not built for it; walks lake and sea floors at 25 mph (40 km); maximum depth 1,000 ft (305 m)', 'Height 19 ft (5.8 m) with the laser cannon level and aimed ahead; width 9.6 ft (3 m); length 7.5 ft (2.3 m)', '16', 300, 1, NULL, 17000000, '17 million credits, solar electric with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell backup, the only factory option; a buyer can have it converted to nuclear afterwards for 1D4+6 million credits, by provider. A replacement solar panel costs 150,000 credits (six in all); holographic decoys 15,000 each.', 'Model Type: NG-SE48 Sunfire. Class: Solar Electric Exploration and Combat Robot. A compact humanoid robot for border and frontier patrol and the defense of remote settlements, from the BGJP R&D group with major work by Scott Kendrick, Albert Rosado, Lawson Stevenson, Mark Dudley and Carl Gleba, and one of NG''s spring 109 P.A. releases. It is NG''s trial of several new systems: six fold-out solar panels charging its electric batteries (never before tried in a full-size combat robot), with a fuel cell held in reserve that a well-tended array should never need; a water-collecting cistern with purification; and holographic decoys to mislead the enemy, so that a canny pilot can stay out for weeks without resupply. The main gun, a short-barreled long-range laser on the right shoulder, draws on the array through a battery of its own; behind the head sits a new variable bore rail gun that takes almost any rail gun round, NG-202 ammunition included, along with Coalition, Triax and foreign types. The pilot and passengers ride in the chest; the head holds the sensor and communications package and two cameras. Small, well armored, cheap and adaptable, it has sold better than NG expected, and buyers are especially taken with the solar system. Head: destroying it takes out every sensor and the radio, and the pilot then sees only through the cameras in the laser cannon and rail gun. Robot P.S. 35. Cargo: a small locker for a rifle, a pistol, a survival kit and a few personal things. Power: solar electric, experimental, with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell backup and an expected life of 12 years; a full charge takes 24 hours of sun with the robot shut down and then runs it at full capacity for 48 hours, so the panels are meant to stay open, soaking up sun on the move or at rest. Hand to hand per Light Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.353) at Robotic P.S. 35. Sensors: the robot standard plus enhanced radar tracking 120 targets to 100 miles (160 km) with a large IFF library that ranks threats, giving +1 initiative and +1 strike to robots, tanks and power armor within a mile (1.6 km) taking its feed, a bonus the Sunfire also gets when it brawls; a scrambled wideband and directional radio relaying audio and video 500 miles (800 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or storms; a directional and all-round microphone hearing a whisper at 400 ft (122 m) and filtering for chosen sounds, with 100 hours of audio storage; four cameras (two in the head, one on each gun) storing 8,000 hours between them and transmitting live or later, through which the pilot sees; and a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.171-174'),
  ('ng-v10-super-labor-original', 'Super Labor Robot NG-V10 "Original"', 'rifts', 'robot', 'One pilot', 'One, very cramped', 'Running 60 mph (96 km) maximum, cruising at 25 mph (40 km), and running does not tire the operator; good on most terrain, including mines, climbs and mountains. Leaping 10 ft (3 m) high or across from a standstill, 20 ft (6 m) high or 30 ft (9 m) across with a run-up. Digging: 1D6x100 cubic yards/meters an hour for a plain hole in soft earth, 1D6x10 an hour of properly shored tunnel, half either rate through stone, concrete or M.D.C. material', 'Not possible', 'Fair: walks lake and sea floors at about half its land speed; maximum depth 2,500 ft (762 m)', 'Height 20 ft (6 m); width 10 ft (3 m); length 8 ft (2.4 m)', '18', 375, 1, NULL, 10000000, 'Nuclear 16 million credits, Solid Oxide Fuel Cell 12 million, electric battery 10 million; the lowest is stored. A civilian version armed only with the laser-claw hand costs 2 million less than the combat model.', 'Model Type: NG-V10. Nicknames: Big Laborer and Super Laborer. Class: Ground Labor Robot Vehicle. A piloted humanoid robot built like an enlarged NG-W10 Heavy Labor Drone, meant for construction and for work where people cannot safely go: mines, lake and sea beds, deserts, toxic sites. Made for mining and building, it has since won over explorers, adventurers, Headhunters and robot pilots who enjoy a brawl. The right hand is an ordinary hand; the left is a three-fingered claw for digging and smashing rock, and the same arm carries a heavy cutting laser. Head: destroying it ruins targeting, sensors and optics, losing every bonus to strike, parry and dodge and leaving the pilot on his own senses. Robot P.S. 38. Cargo: none. Power: nuclear, about 10 years; Solid Oxide Fuel Cell and electric battery models sold too. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), except: restrained punch 1D4 M.D.; full strength punch (right hand) 2D6 M.D.; power punch 4D6 M.D., counting as two attacks; claw strike (left hand) 3D6 M.D.; crush, pry or tear with the claw 3D6 M.D.; dig 2D6 M.D.; power dig 4D6 M.D.; kick 3D8 M.D.; leap kick 5D8 M.D.; stomp 1D4 M.D., only against targets 8 ft (2.4 m) tall or less. Sensors: full optics, with laser targeting, telescopic sight, passive nightvision, thermo-imaging, infrared, ultraviolet and polarized anti-glare filters.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.174-175'),
  ('ng-8000-super-max', 'Super Max NG-8000 Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'One, the pilot', 'None', 'Running 60 mph (96 km) maximum; 90 mph (144 km) mixing running with booster-assisted leaps; scales a building''s side at 40 mph (64 km). Leaping 10 ft (3 m) high and 20 ft (6.1 m) across unaided, 60 ft (18.3 m) high and 100 ft (30.5 m) across with the boosters, and it can drop 1,000 ft (305 m), braked by thrusters, landing on its feet ready to fight or firing on the way down (the text calls that drop roughly ninety stories in one place, nine in another). One main booster lost halves leap height and distance and caps running at 75 mph (120 km); both lost ends boosted leaps and halves its dodge bonus', 'Cannot fly; see the boosted leaps under ground speed', 'Lurches through water on its leap thrusters at 25 mph (40 km), or walks the bottom at the same speed; maximum depth 2,000 ft (610 m)', 'Height 17 ft (5.2 m) head to toe, 21 ft (6.4 m) to the top of the beehive grenade launchers; width 11 ft 6 in (3.5 m); length 9 ft 6 in (2.9 m)', '6.5', 350, 1, NULL, 21000000, '21 million credits list, nuclear, with every built-in weapon, an NG-1001 Robot Rail Rifle and a full load of ammunition; sold out nearly everywhere, so poor availability at list, fair at 50% over it and good at double on secondary markets. Fuel cell (17 million) and battery (14 million) versions were announced for within a year, and NG is weighing a 30% rise on every model.', 'Model Type: NG-8000 Super Max. Class: Infantry Assault Robot. Northern Gun''s reply to the Triax X-1000 Ulti-Max, designed by Ryan Mahoney, Rob Byrne, Dylan Lundrigan, Gil Lee Ba and Ben Rodriguez, and a true giant robot rather than power armor. It is slight for an NG robot, so it carries only light armor, but it is quick, agile and heavily armed. First meant to patrol city streets, parks, plazas and even large building lobbies, it proved suited to any battlefield: it runs, leaps, climbs stairs, walls, trees and cliffs, takes cover, dodges and rolls. Its hands are fine enough to fire Combat Cyborg weapons, set explosives, run most equipment and repair other machines, and most of its weapons sit in the upper torso, so it fights on with its arms gone. A large jet pack on the back and boosters in the shoulders, shins and feet give it balance, lift and huge bounding leaps onto rooftops, across streets and over robots, tanks and walls; reinforced legs and feet survive the landings. An NG advertisement of real field-test footage, a Super Max leaping from a Detroit rooftop into a pack of Brodkil, made it the best seller of NG''s spring 109 P.A. line, and the factory cannot keep up. It is part of NG''s ''upward mobility'' push: robots built for several missions or easily refitted. Head: the NG-CNC99 command and control pod; destroying it ends long-range radio, radar and sensors and costs -2 on all combat bonuses, the pilot then seeing through the chest cameras. Robot P.S. 30. Cargo: a small space for light body armor, a rifle, a pistol, a canteen, four weeks of rations and a first aid kit, plus a built-in water cooler of 2 gallons (7.6 liters). Power: nuclear, 20 year life. Handheld weapons: besides the two rifles in the weapon entries it can use most medium and heavy cyborg guns and robot-sized handheld weapons (Triax makes many), alone or with the NG-1001; NG suggests the NG-404 Heavy Rail Gun or the NG-ML6 Mini-Missile Cannon (Rifts Merc Ops p.94). It also wields large melee weapons such as big Vibro-Swords, or whatever is at hand, magic weapons included. Hand to hand per Light Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.353) at Robotic P.S. 30: restrained punch 1D4 M.D.; full strength punch 2D4 M.D.; power punch 2D6 M.D.; stomp and tear 1D4 M.D.; leap kick 2D8 M.D.; jet boosted leap kick 3D6+2 M.D., only during a jet-assisted leap; the power punch and both leap kicks count as two attacks. Sensors and features: the robot standard plus the NG-CNC99 pod, a sensor, communications, targeting and computer array that makes it a mobile relay and small command post, with enhanced radar tracking 120 targets to 100 miles (160 km) and a large IFF library ranking threats, giving +1 initiative and +1 strike to robots, tanks and power armor within a mile (1.6 km) taking its feed and to itself; enhanced sonar tracking 96 targets to 50 miles (80 km) with the same IFF and relay; a scrambled wideband and directional radio relaying audio and video 500 miles (800 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or storms; a microphone hearing a whisper at 400 ft (122 m) with 100 hours of audio storage; a thermal imager, infrared and ultraviolet optics and passive nightvision, each reaching 2,000 ft (610 m); all of which go with the head. Also a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three; three cameras (head and both sides of the chest) feeding the pilot''s helmet HUD and any designated receiver within 500 miles (800 km), each reaching 4,000 ft (1,219 m) with telescopic and x10 macro lenses and 10,000 hours of storage; and six hardpoints for gear and spare ammunition drums (a pair per leg, one per chest side). Optional NG-A64 Modular Armor Components: clamp-on plates (500,000 credits) adding 40 M.D.C. to the head, 50 to each upper arm and forearm and 60 to each leg, used up before the part beneath, falling away when spent; they cost 25% speed, halve leaps and drops, and give -1 initiative and -1 dodge.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.175-180');

